            <ArticleTitle>Ventricular septal rupture and right ventricular intramyocardial dissection secondary to acute inferior myocardial infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>342-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jcct.2010.05.019</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 61-year-old female patient presented with sub acute myocardial infarction with an occluded right coronary artery on invasive evaluation and a ventricular septal rupture on echocardiogram. Cardiac computed tomography (CT) was performed to better define the septal anatomy. As the anatomy on cardiac CT was considered unfavorable for percutaneous intervention, the patient underwent successful surgical repair.</AbstractText>
